description:
## What this does

The one rule: a move whose target column is protected is refused unless the caller is a live owner or approver of the issue's project. A second, narrower refusal rides along on the same check: a move into a column that merges the pull request is `400` for an issue with no pull request url recorded. Both apply to `MoveIssue`, `PatchIssue` and `BulkEdit` - the three places `IssuesController` changes an issue's `StatusId`.

## Acceptance criterion

`make test-api` is green with: `MoveIssue`, `PatchIssue` and `BulkEdit` each refuse a move whose target column is protected unless the caller is a live owner or approver of the issue's project (`403`, naming the project to a non-member, the program sentence to a key or runner header regardless of membership); each refuses a move into a column with `MergesPullRequest` on for an issue with no `PullRequestUrl` (`400`); a same-column reorder and a move out of a protected column need no membership at all; a bulk edit resolves one key an owner may approve on and one they may not in the same `200`, the second in `failures`; and every one of the 19 existing `new IssuesController(...)` call sites still compiles and its existing tests still pass unchanged. This is acceptance criterion 1 of the parent story, HA-293, sliced to the controller layer alone - the dispatcher fold and the CLI speed bump are HA-293's other two tasks.

## Files

- `src/Hatch.Api/Modules/Hatch/IssuesController.cs` - everything below.
- `src/Hatch.Api/Modules/Hatch/TrunkBuildBugController.cs:71` - one call site to fix (see below).
- `src/Hatch.Api.Tests/Hatch/IssuesControllerTests.cs` - the harness and the new tests.
- 17 other test files construct `IssuesController` directly and need the same one-argument fix (see below) - the build will not compile until every one is touched: `BuildCheckControllerTests.cs`, `AssigneeControllerTests.cs`, `ActivityControllerTests.cs`, `IssueExpressControllerTests.cs`, `IssueExpediteControllerTests.cs`, `ImportControllerTests.cs`, `IssueDependenciesControllerTests.cs`, `IssueStallControllerTests.cs`, `MergeCheckControllerTests.cs`, `IssueWorkLogControllerTests.cs`, `IssueProjectionStallTests.cs`, `IssueWipLimitControllerTests.cs`, `IssuePlaybookControllerTests.cs`, `RunnersControllerTests.cs`, `TrunkBuildControllerTests.cs`, `WipMoveTests.cs`, `WorkLogControllerTests.cs`.

(Confirmed by a literal grep for `new IssuesController(` across the solution: 19 occurrences in 19 files - this list's 17 files, plus `IssuesControllerTests.cs`, plus `TrunkBuildBugController.cs`. Earlier drafts of this ticket undercounted this list by one file; the list above is the corrected, exhaustive one.)

## The constructor

`IssuesController`'s primary constructor (`IssuesController.cs:24-30`) gains `IProjectAccess access` as a new, final parameter. `IProjectAccess` and its real implementation `ProjectAccess` already exist (`ProjectAccess.cs`, landed by HA-290) and are already registered in `HatchModule.cs:52` - no DI change needed, only the constructor list and every direct `new IssuesController(...)` call site (19 of them: the 18 test files above - `IssuesControllerTests.cs` plus the 17 others - plus `TrunkBuildBugController.cs`'s one production call site).

For every test harness, append `new ProjectAccess(db, actors)` (or the harness's own `db`/`actors` variable names) as the seventh argument - the same object `ProjectsControllerTests.cs:708`'s harness already builds for `ProjectsController`, and the same object `IssuesControllerTests.cs:3520` already builds for the `Projects` controller inside *this* harness. None of those 17 other files exercise the new gate (their tests never set a protected column - confirmed by grepping for `IsProtected` across `src/Hatch.Api.Tests/Hatch/*.cs`: the only file that sets it is `StatusesControllerTests.cs`, which does not construct `IssuesController`), so the real implementation is correct there and no stub is needed.

For `TrunkBuildBugController.cs:71`, construct a throwaway `new ProjectAccess(db, actors)` inline next to the existing `IssuesController` construction - that controller only calls `CreateIssueAsync`, which never reads `access`, so there is nothing to wire into `TrunkBuildBugController`'s own constructor for this.

## The two refusals, as one pair of helpers

Add beside `OverrideRefusal`/`ProjectMoveRefusal` ("The one narrowing" region, `IssuesController.cs:1007` on):

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// A live owner or approver of the project, and never a program - the one
/// status-transition rule Hatch has (docs/hatch.md, "Non-goals"). Null
/// wherever <paramref name="target"/> is not protected, so neither the
/// program check nor the membership read happens on an ordinary move.
/// </summary>
private async Task<string?> ProtectedMoveRefusalAsync(EfHatchProject project, EfHatchStatus target, CancellationToken ct)
{
    if (!target.IsProtected) return null;

    if (await caller.IsProgramAsync(ct))
        return "moving work into a protected column is an owner's or approver's call, not an agent's";

    if (!await access.CanApproveAsync(project.Id, ct))
        return $"only an owner or approver of {project.Key} may move work into \"{target.Name}\"";

    return null;
}

/// <summary>400 for a move into a column that merges the pull request, when the issue carries none.</summary>
private static string? MergeColumnRefusal(EfHatchIssue issue, EfHatchStatus target) =>
    target.MergesPullRequest && string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(issue.PullRequestUrl)
        ? $"\"{target.Name}\" merges the pull request, and this issue has none recorded"
        : null;
```

Both return a plain sentence (or null), the same shape `Error`/`Conflict` already use inside `StageEditAsync` - `ProtectedMoveRefusalAsync` is not itself an `ActionResult`; each call site below wraps its non-null result in whatever HTTP shape that site needs (a `403 ObjectResult` in `MoveIssue`, a plain tuple slot in `StageEditAsync`). `ProtectedMoveRefusalAsync` takes the project to check membership against, not `issue.ProjectId` directly - see the project-move note below.

## `MoveIssue` (`IssuesController.cs:883-935`)

After `var changedColumn = issue.StatusId != status.Id;` and before the `if (changedColumn)` block's existing body, insert, still inside that `if`:

```csharp
if (await ProtectedMoveRefusalAsync(issue.Project!, status, ct) is { } forbidden)
    return new ObjectResult(forbidden) { StatusCode = StatusCodes.Status403Forbidden };
if (MergeColumnRefusal(issue, status) is { } mergeRefusal) return BadRequest(mergeRefusal);
```

before the `var now = time.GetUtcNow();` / `WipGate` lines that follow today. `issue.Project` is already loaded (`LoadAsync` includes it). A same-column reorder never reaches this block at all (`changedColumn` is false), which is what leaves reordering and moving-out ungated, by construction - no extra code for either.

## `StageEditAsync` (`IssuesController.cs:596` on), shared by `PatchIssue` and `BulkEdit`

Its return tuple (`IssuesController.cs:596-598`) gains a sixth element, appended: `string? Forbidden` - so the full signature reads `(bool Changed, string? Error, string? Conflict, WipRefusalDto? Full, IReadOnlyList<(string From, string To)> Rekeyed, string? Forbidden)`. Every existing `return (...)` in the method gains a trailing `, null` (or `, forbidden` where this section writes it) to match the new arity - there are several early returns above the status block (project lookup, live-claim-on-move) that stay `null` here.

Inside the existing status block (`IssuesController.cs:643-648`):

```csharp
EfHatchStatus? status = null;
if (edit.StatusId is { } statusId && statusId != issue.StatusId)
{
    status = await db.Statuses.FirstOrDefaultAsync(s => s.Id == statusId, ct);
    if (status is null) return (false, $"there is no column {statusId}", null, null, [], null);

    // The project this status change is judged against: the one the issue
    // is moving into, when this same edit also moves projects, else the
    // one it already stands in - an issue's approval authority follows
    // where it is going to live, not where it is leaving.
    var statusProject = targetProject ?? issue.Project!;
    if (await ProtectedMoveRefusalAsync(statusProject, status, ct) is { } forbidden)
        return (false, null, null, null, [], forbidden);
    if (MergeColumnRefusal(issue, status) is { } mergeRefusal)
        return (false, mergeRefusal, null, null, [], null);
}
```

`targetProject` is the local already resolved a few lines above the status block (`IssuesController.cs:608-614`) - null unless this same edit also moves projects.

## `PatchIssue` (`IssuesController.cs:342-414`), two call sites

Both destructure `StageEditAsync`'s result. Add `forbidden` to the destructure and check it first, ahead of `full`/`conflict`/`error`:

```csharp
var (changed, error, conflict, full, _, forbidden) = await StageEditAsync(...);
if (forbidden is not null) return new ObjectResult(forbidden) { StatusCode = StatusCodes.Status403Forbidden };
if (full is not null) return Conflict(full);
if (conflict is not null) return Conflict(conflict);
if (error is not null) return BadRequest(error);
```

Once in the non-moving branch (`:369-374`), once in the mint-retry loop (`:392-396`).

## `BulkEdit` (`IssuesController.cs:434-575`), one call site

Inside `ResolveAndStageAsync` (`:519-525`), add `forbidden` to the destructure and report it into `failures` exactly as `error`/`conflict`/`full.Error` already are - this is what makes the refusal per-key rather than whole-request, so one issue in a project the caller cannot approve on does not refuse the other ninety-nine in the same bulk edit:

```csharp
var (moved, error, conflict, full, rekeyedByThis, forbidden) = await StageEditAsync(...);
if (forbidden is not null) failures.Add(new IssueBulkFailureDto(key, forbidden));
else if (full is not null) failures.Add(new IssueBulkFailureDto(key, full.Error));
else if (conflict is not null) failures.Add(new IssueBulkFailureDto(key, conflict));
else if (error is not null) failures.Add(new IssueBulkFailureDto(key, error));
else if (moved) changed.Add(await KeyOfAsync(issue, ct));
else unchanged.Add(await KeyOfAsync(issue, ct));
```

## Tests, in `IssuesControllerTests.cs`

`NewAsync` (`:3490-3528`) needs a protected column and a merge-protected column beside the existing `inbox`/`todo`/`done`, e.g.:

```csharp
var approved = new EfHatchStatus { Name = "approved", SortOrder = 30, IsProtected = true };
var merges = new EfHatchStatus { Name = "merges", SortOrder = 35, IsProtected = true, MergesPullRequest = true };
```

added to the `db.AddRange(...)` call, with `Approved`/`Merges` ids exposed on the `Harness` record the way `Inbox`/`Todo`/`Done` already are (`:3376-3380`). Change `Issues = new IssuesController(db, ranks, actors, TestClaims.With(), caller, time)` (`:3516`) to append `, new ProjectAccess(db, actors)`.

Note on this file's own `StubCallerIdentity` (private, `:3531-3556`): it is **not** the same class as `src/Hatch.Api.Tests/Auth/AuthFakes.cs`'s `StubCallerIdentity`, which happens to share the name but spells its key field `ApiKey`. This file's own copy spells it `Key` (`:3536`), and its `IsProgramAsync` (`:3551-3552`) reads `Key is not null || Local is { Kind: ActorKind.Key }`. Use `Key`, not `ApiKey`, in every test below - the field name the tests need is whatever `StubCallerIdentity` actually exposes in *this* file.

Write a new test region ("Protected columns", near the existing move/patch/bulk tests, e.g. after `## ---- Moving between projects ----` at `:661` or `## ---- Moving ----` at `:1220`) covering the acceptance criterion above, one case per fact - model each on the patterns already in this codebase rather than inventing a new one:

- **Owner allowed, approver allowed**: `var owner = h.Actors.AddPerson("Ada");` then `h.Db.ProjectMembers.Add(new EfHatchProjectMember { ProjectId = h.ProjectId, PersonId = owner.Id, Role = ProjectMemberRole.Owner /* a second fact: .Approver */, CreatedAt = Now }); await h.Db.SaveChangesAsync();` and `h.Actors.Me = owner;` - only `Actors.Me` matters here, because `ProjectAccess.HasRoleAsync` resolves identity through `actors.MeAsync()`, never through `caller.Person`; `h.Caller.Person` can stay the harness's default "Nathan" unchanged (it is a different type than `Actor` and is not read by the gate at all). Then `MoveIssue`/`PatchIssue`/`BulkEdit` into the protected column succeeds (200, and the issue's `StatusId` is the protected column's).
- **Another person is 403, naming the project**: a different live person added to `Actors` and set as `Actors.Me`, no membership row - assert the `ObjectResult`'s `StatusCode` is 403 and its `Value` is the exact sentence naming `h.ProjectId`'s key (`"AER"` in this harness).
- **A key is 403**: `h.Caller.Key = AProgram();` (the existing helper at `:172`, already used this exact way by `AKeyOrKeylessRunnerMovingAProject_Is403AndWritesNothing` at `:687-699`) - 403 with the program sentence, regardless of membership. No need to touch `Person`.
- **Local operator passes with the wall off**: `h.Caller.Local = new Actor(ActorKind.Person, LocalCaller.PersonId, "Ada")`, `h.Actors.Me = new Actor(ActorKind.Person, LocalCaller.PersonId, "Ada")`, and a membership row for `LocalCaller.PersonId` - succeeds. This is the `IssueClaimTests.cs:552` / `WipMoveTests.cs:221` idiom, applied to ownership instead of WIP; neither existing instance nulls `Caller.Person` first, and it is not necessary here either, since `IsProgramAsync` never reads it.
- **Runner header is 403**: `h.Caller.Local = new Actor(ActorKind.Key, Guid.NewGuid(), "a-runner")` (mirrors `WipMoveTests.cs:221` exactly) - 403 with the program sentence even with a membership row present, because `IsProgramAsync` is checked first and unconditionally.
- **No pull request url into a merge column is 400**: move/patch/bulk an issue with `PullRequestUrl = null` into `merges` as an owner - `400` with the merge sentence; set `PullRequestUrl` and the same move succeeds.
- **Same-column reorder is allowed**: seed an issue already sitting in `approved` by creating it normally (`h.CreateAsync`, which lands in `Inbox`) and then writing `StatusId` directly on the tracked entity and saving - the same direct-db-write-as-setup idiom already used at `:839-850` - since getting the issue into `approved` through the controller would itself require membership, which this test deliberately has none of. With it seeded, move it between two neighbours still inside `approved` with no membership row at all - succeeds. Mirrors `AMoveWithinAColumn_WritesNothing` (`:1240-1252`), the existing same-column-reorder test for the unprotected case.
- **A move out is allowed**: seed an issue in `approved` the same direct-db way, then move it to `done` (not protected), with no membership row - succeeds.
- One bulk-specific case: two keys in the same bulk request, one in a project the caller may approve on and one not - the first lands in `changed`/`unchanged`, the second in `failures` with the project-naming sentence, and the whole call still returns `200`.

## What was checked

Read `IssuesController.cs` in full for `MoveIssue`, `PatchIssue`, `BulkEdit`, `StageEditAsync`, `OverrideRefusal`, `ProjectMoveRefusal` and `LoadAsync` (confirms `issue.Project` is always loaded), and re-verified every line number above against the file as it stands today. Read `ProjectAccess.cs` and confirmed `CanApproveAsync`/`IsOwnerAsync` already exist exactly as HA-293 expects, landed by HA-290 and already wired into `HatchModule.cs:52`. Read `StatusesController.cs` and `Entities.cs` and confirmed `EfHatchStatus.IsProtected`/`MergesPullRequest` already exist and are already read by `StatusDto`, landed by HA-292 - this story only reads them, never writes them. Confirmed `EfHatchProjectMember` (`Entities.cs:177-198`) and `ProjectMemberRole.{Owner,Approver}` (`:163-169`) have exactly the shape the test setup above uses. Read `ProjectsController.cs`'s `PutMember`/`NotAPerson` for the 403-sentence idiom this mirrors, and `CallerIdentity.cs`'s `IsProgramAsync`/`LocalAsync` for exactly how the wall-off local operator (`Local.Kind == Person`) and a runner header (`Local.Kind == Key`) are already told apart - no new auth plumbing needed, only calling what exists. Grepped every direct `new IssuesController(...)` call site: **19** total (confirmed by `rg -c`, not estimated), corrected from this ticket's own earlier miscount of 18 - see "Files" above. Read `IssuesControllerTests.cs`'s own private `StubCallerIdentity` (`:3531-3556`) field-by-field and confirmed it is a different class from `AuthFakes.cs`'s same-named one; the field this file needs is `Key`, not `ApiKey` - an earlier draft of this ticket's test spec had borrowed the wrong class's field name, which would not have compiled. Read `StubActorDirectory.cs` and confirmed `Me`/`AddPerson` are independent, settable members a test can use directly. Grepped `IsProtected` across every other test file in `src/Hatch.Api.Tests/Hatch/` and confirmed none of the 17 other `IssuesController`-constructing files ever sets it, so the real `ProjectAccess` is safe to wire into all of them with no stub. Read `AMoveWithinAColumn_WritesNothing` (`:1240-1252`) and the direct-db issue seeding at `:839-850` as the exact models for the two "already in a protected column" setup steps above. Confirmed `MaxBulkKeys` is `500` (`IssuesController.cs:45`). This is one cohesive, file-disjoint slice of HA-293 already (the controller, its one production caller, and its tests) with no further internal seam worth cutting into child tasks - everything above lands as one commit.

## Not checked

Whether `access.CanApproveAsync` issuing up to two queries per bulk key is a real cost at `MaxBulkKeys` (500) was not measured - `LoadAsync` already costs one query per key in this same loop today, so this does not change the bulk editor's existing per-key-query shape, and no batching was designed for it.
